1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,200
At the end of a white glacier in Antarctica, blood-red water flows out.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,800
It's Blood Falls, at Taylor Glacier.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:11,250
Explorers first saw it in 1911. They thought red algae made the color.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:14,850
The real reason was very salty water trapped under the glacier.

5
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:19,200
It's many times saltier than seawater, so it doesn't freeze below zero.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:25,050
It's full of iron. When it meets air, it rusts and turns red.

7
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:28,600
When it comes out of the glacier, it's actually clear.

8
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:33,150
In that dark water, tiny life lives with no light and no oxygen.
